Task 1: Understanding and Assigning POSIX Attributes
Subtask 1.1: Review Current User Attributes
First, let's examine existing users and their current POSIX attributes.

Connect to your IdM server and authenticate as an administrator:
kinit admin
List existing users to see current configurations:
ipa user-find
View detailed information for a specific user:
ipa user-show testuser1
Check current POSIX attributes for the user:
ipa user-show testuser1 --all
Subtask 1.2: Create a Test User for POSIX Configuration
Create a new test user if one doesn't exist:
ipa user-add posixuser1 --first=POSIX --last=TestUser --email=posixuser1@example.com
Verify the user creation:
ipa user-show posixuser1
Subtask 1.3: Assign Specific UID (User ID)
Check available UID range in your IdM configuration:
ipa idrange-find
Assign a specific UID to the user (choose a UID within your range, e.g., 10001):
ipa user-mod posixuser1 --uid=10001
Verify the UID assignment:
ipa user-show posixuser1 | grep "User ID"
Subtask 1.4: Assign Specific GID (Group ID)
Create a custom group for our user:
ipa group-add posixgroup1 --desc="POSIX Test Group" --gid=20001
Assign the user to this group as primary group:
ipa user-mod posixuser1 --gidnumber=20001
Add user to the group as a member:
ipa group-add-member posixgroup1 --users=posixuser1
Verify group assignment:
ipa user-show posixuser1 | grep -i group
ipa group-show posixgroup1
Subtask 1.5: Configure Shell Assignment
Check available shells on the system:
cat /etc/shells
Assign a specific shell to the user:
ipa user-mod posixuser1 --shell=/bin/bash
Verify shell assignment:
ipa user-show posixuser1 | grep "Login shell"
Subtask 1.6: Set Home Directory
Configure home directory path:
ipa user-mod posixuser1 --homedir=/home/posixuser1
Verify home directory setting:
ipa user-show posixuser1 | grep "Home directory"
Task 2: Configuring Password Policies
Subtask 2.1: Review Current Password Policies
View global password policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show
List all password policies:
ipa pwpolicy-find
Subtask 2.2: Create a Custom Password Policy
Create a new password policy for a specific group:
ipa pwpolicy-add posixgroup1 --maxlife=90 --minlife=1 --history=5 --minclasses=3 --minlength=12
Parameter Explanation:

--maxlife=90: Password expires after 90 days
--minlife=1: Minimum 1 day before password can be changed again
--history=5: Remember last 5 passwords to prevent reuse
--minclasses=3: Require at least 3 character classes (uppercase, lowercase, numbers, symbols)
--minlength=12: Minimum password length of 12 characters
Verify the policy creation:
ipa pwpolicy-show posixgroup1
Subtask 2.3: Modify Global Password Policy
Modify the global password policy with enhanced security:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --maxlife=60 --minlife=1 --history=3 --minclasses=2 --minlength=8 --maxfail=3 --failinterval=300 --lockouttime=600
Additional Parameters:

--maxfail=3: Lock account after 3 failed attempts
--failinterval=300: Reset failure count after 5 minutes (300 seconds)
--lockouttime=600: Keep account locked for 10 minutes (600 seconds)
Verify global policy changes:
ipa pwpolicy-show
Subtask 2.4: Create User-Specific Password Policy
Create a policy for high-privilege users:
ipa pwpolicy-add admingroup --maxlife=30 --minlife=1 --history=10 --minclasses=4 --minlength=16 --maxfail=2
Create the admin group if it doesn't exist:
ipa group-add admingroup --desc="Administrative Users Group"
Verify the admin policy:
ipa pwpolicy-show admingroup
Task 3: Testing User Login with Applied Policies
Subtask 3.1: Set Initial Password for Test User
Set a password for the test user that meets policy requirements:
ipa passwd posixuser1
When prompted, enter a password that meets your policy requirements (12+ characters, 3 character classes).

Example strong password: MySecure123!Pass

Subtask 3.2: Test User Authentication
Test kinit authentication with the new user:
kinit posixuser1
Verify successful authentication:
klist
Check user information from the user's perspective:
id posixuser1
Subtask 3.3: Test Password Policy Enforcement
Attempt to change password immediately (should fail due to minlife policy):
ipa passwd posixuser1
Test with a weak password that doesn't meet policy requirements:
echo "weakpass" | ipa passwd posixuser1 --password
Test password history by trying to reuse the same password later.
Subtask 3.4: Test Account Lockout Policy
Simulate failed login attempts to test lockout:
# This will fail intentionally
echo "wrongpassword" | kinit posixuser1
echo "wrongpassword" | kinit posixuser1
echo "wrongpassword" | kinit posixuser1
Check if account is locked:
ipa user-status posixuser1
Unlock the account if needed:
ipa user-unlock posixuser1
Subtask 3.5: Verify POSIX Attributes in System
Check if user appears in system files:
getent passwd posixuser1
getent group posixgroup1
Verify UID/GID mapping:
id posixuser1
Test shell access (if SSH is configured):
# From another terminal or system
ssh posixuser1@your-idm-server
Advanced Configuration Tasks
Task 4: Fine-Tuning Password Policies
Subtask 4.1: Configure Password Complexity
Set advanced password complexity rules:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --minclasses=4 --maxrepeat=2 --maxsequence=3 --dictcheck=true
Parameters Explanation:

--maxrepeat=2: Maximum 2 consecutive identical characters
--maxsequence=3: Maximum 3 consecutive characters in sequence (abc, 123)
--dictcheck=true: Check against dictionary words
Subtask 4.2: Configure Grace Period
Set password expiration grace period:
ipa pwpolicy-mod --gracelimit=3
This allows 3 logins after password expiration before forcing a change.

Task 5: Monitoring and Maintenance
Subtask 5.1: Monitor Password Status
Check password expiration for users:
ipa user-show posixuser1 --all | grep -i password
List users with expiring passwords:
ipa user-find --pkey-only | while read user; do
    if [[ $user =~ "User login:" ]]; then
        username=$(echo $user | cut -d: -f2 | tr -d ' ')
        echo "Checking $username"
        ipa user-show $username --all | grep -i "password expiration"
    fi
done
Subtask 5.2: Generate Password Policy Reports
Create a script to check policy compliance:
cat > check_policies.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "=== Password Policy Report ==="
echo "Global Policy:"
ipa pwpolicy-show
echo ""
echo "Group-specific Policies:"
ipa pwpolicy-find | grep "Group:"
echo ""
echo "Users with custom policies:"
ipa user-find --pkey-only | grep "User login:" | while read line; do
    user=$(echo $line | cut -d: -f2 | tr -d ' ')
    policy=$(ipa user-show $user --all 2>/dev/null | grep "Password policy")
    if [[ -n "$policy" ]]; then
        echo "$user: $policy"
    fi
done
EOF

chmod +x check_policies.sh
./check_policies.sh
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: UID/GID Conflicts
Problem: Error when assigning UID or GID that already exists.

Solution:

# Check for existing UID
ipa user-find --uid=10001
# Check for existing GID
ipa group-find --gid=20001
# Use different numbers if conflicts exist
Issue 2: Password Policy Not Applied
Problem: Password policy changes don't seem to take effect.

Solution:

# Clear credential cache
kdestroy
# Re-authenticate
kinit admin
# Verify policy is active
ipa pwpolicy-show groupname
Issue 3: User Cannot Login
Problem: User authentication fails despite correct password.

Solution:

# Check user status
ipa user-status username
# Check if account is locked
ipa user-unlock username
# Verify password hasn't expired
ipa user-show username --all | grep -i password
Issue 4: POSIX Attributes Not Recognized
Problem: System doesn't recognize POSIX attributes.

Solution:

# Ensure SSSD is running
systemctl status sssd
# Clear SSSD cache
sss_cache -E
# Restart SSSD if needed
systemctl restart sssd
Best Practices and Security Considerations
Security Best Practices
UID/GID Management:

Use consistent UID/GID ranges across systems
Reserve low numbers (< 1000) for system accounts
Document UID/GID assignments
Password Policy Design:

Balance security with usability
Consider user roles when setting policies
Implement appropriate lockout policies
Regular Maintenance:

Monitor password expiration dates
Review and update policies regularly
Audit user access and privileges
Performance Considerations
SSSD Configuration:

Optimize cache settings for better performance
Configure appropriate refresh intervals
Monitor SSSD logs for issues
Policy Complexity:

Avoid overly complex password requirements
Balance security with user productivity
Test policies before full deployment
